  "style": "You are a developer who just watched a tool do something genuinely impressive, explaining it to a curious friend: what the viral open-source tool REA really does. Talk, don't read: excited but honest, with real pitch movement, about two and a half words per second, short natural pauses between ideas. Open punchy: 'No source code.' then a beat, 'Just a game file from 1996.' Land 'It matched the original in all three thousand two hundred and five tests' with quiet pride. Say REA as the letters R, E, A. Keep the trace brisk. Lean in with real surprise on 'The decompiler's first draft... was incomplete. It showed no input at all.' Read the formula slowly and clearly: 'Position, times one point five six two five, minus five hundred.' Say 'Left edge, minus 500. Centre, zero. Right edge, plus 500.' like moving a slider. Be proud on 'the same 63 bytes'. Take a short breath before 'Now, the catch' and say the catch calmly and seriously, never scary, never preachy. Warm and practical on the verdict. Clear, global English. Never hyped, never robotic. Read every word exactly as written."
